          "question": "Compare and contrast the features of a Sole Proprietorship and a General Partnership in India, focusing on ownership, liability, and regulatory framework.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "### Model Answer (10 Marks)\n\n**1. Ownership Structure (3 Marks):**\nA Sole Proprietorship is defined by single ownership, where one natural person (the proprietor) owns 100% of the business. In contrast, a General Partnership requires a minimum of two partners and a maximum of fifty. The owners in a partnership are termed \"partners.\" While a sole proprietor has exclusive control, partnership ownership is shared, although management can be delegated.\n\n**2. Liability Feature (3 Marks):**\nBoth business forms share the characteristic of **unlimited liability**. In a sole proprietorship, the proprietor's personal assets are fully exposed to business creditors. In a general partnership, partners have \"joint and several liability.\" This means that a creditor can sue any one partner for the full amount of the debt, or all partners collectively. If one partner pays the entire debt, they can seek contribution from the other partners. Personal wealth of partners is at risk, similar to the sole proprietor.\n\n**3. Regulatory Framework (4 Marks):**\nA critical distinction examined in exams is the legal regulation. In India, a Sole Proprietorship is not governed by any specific codified Act; it operates under general laws such as the GST Act, Shops and Establishments Act, and municipal regulations. Conversely, a General Partnership is specifically regulated under the **Indian Partnership Act, 1932**. This Act provides the default rules for the relationship between partners. Furthermore, partners can create a \"Partnership Deed\" a contractual document that can vary or override many of the default provisions of the 1932 Act, such as profit-sharing ratios and the duration of the firm.\n\n**4. Business Life (Optional/4 Marks):**\nBoth forms have limited life. A sole proprietorship terminates upon the death or insolvency of the proprietor. A general partnership typically dissolves upon the death, retirement, or insolvency of a partner, unless a specific clause in the Partnership Deed provides for the firm's continuation or the admission of a new partner.\n\n**Conclusion:** While both are simple to form and operate with minimal compliance compared to companies, the sole proprietorship is the simplest form with one owner and no specific Act, whereas the partnership allows for shared ownership (up to 50) but is strictly regulated by the Indian Partnership Act, 1932, and carries inherent unlimited liability for all members.",
